// The boss journal: Hrimgar the Frost Titan, Warden of the White Reach, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).titan = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Nobody drilled me for ground that lets go on his say. I have held mud, stairs and a burning deck. He is slow, I will report that much; the floor is what I fell through. Gave ground, more than once, and not to him.",
    gloomsworn: "A giant keeping a night watch under green lights, and nobody coming to relieve him. I know that look. The ice broke under us and the black water was no stranger. I have been colder. Not by much.",
    thresher: "He is magnificent. A whole country gone to hard frost and him standing in the middle of it, like the first thing ever made. I hurried across his ice and it dropped me in the black water. In time, I told myself, wet to the neck. Not yet, apparently.",
    bloodreaver: "Hah! He hits like a falling barn and I loved him for it. Then the floor gave way and I met the black water, which does not hit back, only takes. No scar to show for it. That is a grudge. I will be back, big one.",
    runeguard: "Each blow is carved on the ice an instant before it falls, the most courteous thing I have seen a giant do. The floor, though, keeps writing after. I read the blows well and the ground badly. Margin: learn the ground.",
    gorgemaw: "Chilled courses on a table of ice, every plate bigger than me. Slow service, but I do like a generous host. Then the table gave way and I went down with the soup. Cold, wet, hungry. Seconds, please, from somewhere drier.",
    oathbroker: "Hrimgar holds the Reach by a very old charter and enforces it in plain sight, every term drawn on the ice before it falls. What I failed to read was the floor: whatever he cracks, he keeps. I paid in a wetting. Breach noted.",
    ironpalm: "He speaks slowly and shows each word before saying it. I listened so hard to him that I forgot the ice was speaking too. It said sink, and I went. Cold water is also a teacher. I came back to the plateau quieter.",
    ranger: "Big. Slow. Shows every swing. Ground is the problem: it cracks, it bites, glazed patches slide you off. Lost a boot in the black water; won't say which. Watch your feet, not the bull.",
    shadowblade: "Enormous, slow, and he announces everything on the floor in advance, which I find rather sweet. Then the ice dropped me into water that was frankly insulting, and spoiled a perfectly good entrance. Not twice, darling.",
    pyromancer: "A Snowman! An enormous grumpy Snowman, and I am the only warm thing on the mountain! The braziers were wonderful. My eyebrows froze solid, which has never once happened, and I would like it noted that I did not enjoy it.",
    thermist: "Fascinating! Cold, all the way down, and I ran cold all night. Result: his blows are slow and the ice is fast. The braziers hummed at me like home, and the one time I strayed from one, I seized. Back to the middle.",
    driftblade: "He is a mountain that walks, slow enough to circle all night, and I did, and the ice slid me wider than I meant and into black water nobody had mentioned. Even hawks get wet. Next time I watch the floor under my feet.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man is vast and deliberate, every gesture shown to the house in advance. The stage, alas, is not sound: it gives way. Nobody warned the company. (My Doll got wet and is sulking in the wings.)",
    cleric: "Poor Hrimgar, so old and so cold, and so patient about showing us each blow. It was the ice that betrayed us. I pulled two of my young ones out of the black water by their collars. Dawn keep us. Hot tea, then back to it.",
    grovekeeper: "Old Winter, standing up at last, dear. He walks the way frost comes, slow and sure, and the ground goes hard or goes away. I misread him; I watched the sky and not the soil. The braziers were the only warm earth up there.",
    chronomancer: "He is so slow that I kept moving early, and then late. Each crack comes in its own time and I did not know the times. I will have learned them by now. I did not, quite. Remind me. No, do not.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one giant of ice, slow, large, labelled in advance by the floor. Everything in my satchel froze solid by the second blow and one flask cracked, loudly. Not my fault. Possibly my fault. Dry the shelves.",
    threadweaver: "The great lump is slow as a winter evening, but the floor under him comes apart like a rotten hem. My stitches slid and sank in every direction. A sloppy seam, and the sloppiness was mine. Measure the ice before pinning anyone to it.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The Titan is down. The plateau is quiet, the lights hang over it, the road north is open. I stood on bad ice the whole fight and the line never broke. Report ends here. I am going to sit down.",
    gloomsworn: "The giant has laid down his watch, and I stood the last of it for him. The lights hang over the plateau, cold and green. No one came to relieve him either. The road north is open. The watch is relieved.",
    thresher: "Hrimgar has come in at last, and the Reach may sleep beneath its own snow. The lights were the colour of pale fire. Good ground to rest in. The road north is open. Something in me is nearly due.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one fell like a mountain and the whole plateau shook! Every bruise he gave me is still cold and blue, the best I ever took. Thank you, my friend. Now the road north, and whatever waits up there.",
    runeguard: "The Frost Titan is down and the record of his anger is closed. Every blow he carved I read in time; the floor I read late, and that is entered as well, for my successor. The lights went quiet as a reading room.",
    gorgemaw: "The ice course is done. My host fell into his own pantry and the whole plateau went silent. Cold, clean, very slightly bitter, and magnificent. The road north promises warmer kitchens. I am still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "The Warden's title to the Reach is void and the road north passes without toll. Every levy he drew on the ice we paid; the last instalment came due on him. Countersigned, in frost.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke his last word, slowly, and we answered. Then the plateau was quiet and the lights moved on overhead. I stood in the snow and my breath rose, and I let it.",
    ranger: "Old bull's down. Lights over the ice. Road north clear as a trail in fresh snow. Boots dry at the end, which I'll take. Quiet night.",
    shadowblade: "The mark lay down on its own ice and stayed there. The plateau went so quiet I could hear the lights. It never saw me, darling, not once, and now it never will.",
    pyromancer: "I beat the Snowman! The lights were glorious and I was the warmest thing under them. My eyebrows have thawed and so have my sleeves. Mostly unsinged, and for once, mostly unfrozen.",
    thermist: "He fell and the cold stopped pressing. Green lights, slow, overhead. I stood between the braziers and the dark, exactly at the edge, neither hot nor cold. Result: success. Back to the middle.",
    driftblade: "He fell and the plateau rang like a bell and I ran out across the ice under the lights and up the road north and never once slid into the water. Not once. Ask anyone.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for the star of the ice. The house lights were the sky itself, green and moving. The company bowed, the Doll lowest. (Exit north, all of us, to rapturous silence.)",
    cleric: "He lies still at last, old Hrimgar, and the lights are soft over him. Everyone is breathing. That was all I asked. The road north is open; may the dawn find it kind.",
    grovekeeper: "Old Winter's gone to ground, dear, and the snow will settle on him like a blanket. Spring has to come from somewhere. The lights were lovely, and every one of my lot walked out of it.",
    chronomancer: "He fell. I will have known he would. The lights moved overhead, taking their time, the way I should have. The road north is open, and nobody had to be undone at the end. Nearly nobody.",
    alembist: "Specimen defeated! The lights were a lovely reaction, green and slow. I salvaged two flasks and one eyebrow. Road north open. Label for the shelf: do not store in the cold. Do not store near me.",
    threadweaver: "He's down. The lights hung over the plateau like a bolt of silk shaken out, and every stitch of mine was still tied to me, sound at the seams. The road north is open. Put the kettle on.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Winter's Howl": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Nothing to block, nothing to hold. Sergeant's rule for a thing like that: be in good order before it arrives. The healers had us stitched up before he drew breath, and I stood in the open and took it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The howl reaches every one of us, and the dark has no shelter from it. I made sure the watch was whole before his chest rose, then let the cold pass through me like the small hours. It always passes.",
      thresher: "All of us at once, a killing frost with nowhere to hide. Best I can do is arrive full. I did, and kept swinging through it. It is only weather.",
      bloodreaver: "The howl went through my bones, and my friends' too, and they felt it worse than I did. Barely a scratch for me, shameful. So we were mended up before it came, and nobody fell. I'll hurt myself properly later.",
      runeguard: "His chest swelled and the lights above flickered, sign enough. It reaches all of us and no ward turns it; the only answer is to be whole before it sounds. Entered in the margin: some things are met, not stopped.",
      gorgemaw: "My host serves the howl to the whole table, iced, and I cannot eat it for anyone else. So the guests came to the table well fed and swallowed it together. A sharp, thin sort of dish. Brain-freezing.",
      oathbroker: "A levy on every one of the undersigned, payable at once, with no exemption in the text. The only provision is to arrive already mended. We did, and the howl cleared without default. Entered.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to all of us at once, coldly. There is no answer to that, only readiness. We came to it whole and let it pass through, as a wind through an open door.",
      ranger: "Howl. No cover anywhere, nowhere to run. Came to it mended and kept the bow drawn through it. Frost on my string after.",
      shadowblade: "The howl found everyone, even me, which I consider rude. No shadow is deep enough to keep it out, so I made sure I was whole when it came, then got back to work.",
      pyromancer: "He howled and my hair froze stiff as a candle! There is nowhere to escape it, so I was topped up beforehand and kept casting through it, purely out of spite. It thawed by dawn. Mostly.",
      thermist: "Howl. Cold, every one of us. Be whole before it comes. Then it hit and I shivered to the marrow, wild with it, and burned it off with the next three casts. Hypothesis confirmed: cold goes where I stand.",
      driftblade: "He howled across the whole plateau and I couldn't run round it or through it, which I resent very much, so I came to it whole and kept moving through the sound and out the other side of it.",
      marionettist: "Our star filled the whole house with his howl, stalls and gallery alike, and the exits were painted on. The company arrived whole and played on. (The Doll's paint cracked a little. A small price for the notices.)",
      cleric: "The howl fell on every one of us. I did my mending before he raised his head, and not after, and laid a little warmth over them as it came. They came through grumbling, which is the best sign.",
      grovekeeper: "It comes like a hard frost overnight, dear, no cover, all of my lot at once. I planted the mending in each of them before it broke, and it was growing when the cold came down.",
      chronomancer: "The howl lands one beat after his chest fills. I had everyone mended in the moment before, which is a good place to be. Last time, which was next time, I was a beat late and regretted it.",
      alembist: "Observation: the howl, felt by everyone, nothing to hide behind. Dose everyone well before it sounds and keep the strong flask uncorked for right after. Shelf kept warm. Mostly warm.",
      threadweaver: "The howl ran along every thread at once, and a cold that size can't be tied off. So I made sure we stood whole and close, and pulled us in tight so it ran through all of us evenly. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Glacial Fist': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The fist was mine alone. Sergeant always said watch the shoulder, not the hand. Shield up in the last instant, and the rest of the line stayed out of the wedge, as ordered.",
      gloomsworn: "The fist was meant for whoever keeps the front, and that was me. I drew the dark about me just before it fell and took it whole. The others stood outside the wedge, where the night is kinder.",
      thresher: "Down it comes on our knight, a wedge as wide as a millstone. I kept to his flank and kept to my place. Anyone who wanders into the front of him is clipped, and it is never a clean cut.",
      bloodreaver: "The fist! Square on me, a wedge of ice in front of him, and it rattled every tooth I have. A good one. I braced, took it, and hit back while it was still ringing. The others kept clear, bless them.",
      runeguard: "His arm drew back, and the wedge before him was drawn upon the ice, a courtesy. I set a ward upon whoever stands in it and had everyone else outside it, since it clips the careless. The sequence is plain once you have read it once.",
      gorgemaw: "The fist is the main course, served to one guest, and a great wedge of it. I opened wide just before and kept my friends off my plate. A little crunchy, as all ice is. Needs salt.",
      oathbroker: "The Fist is payable by whoever stands before him. I took delivery behind every protection I had. Fine print: the wedge is wide, and anyone left in it is billed a smaller share. Surety given.",
      ironpalm: "He struck once, wide, at the one before him. I did not lift my arms until its last instant, and I took half. The others stood aside. Then I answered him.",
      ranger: "Fist. Wide wedge in front of it, meant for our knight. Kept to its flank, out of the wedge. Strays get clipped.",
      shadowblade: "It hammered whoever faced it, which was not me, which is never me. I kept to its side, out of the wedge, and returned when the ice had settled. Darling, it is a matter of manners.",
      pyromancer: "He punched the ice in front of him and I decided I did not need to be there! I stood off his side, out of the wedge, and let our knight have all the glory. Not a flicker out of place.",
      thermist: "Fist: cold, wide, in front. Our knight braces; I step aside. Then hot, swinging back in at once from his side and not his face, delighted with myself. A very tidy experiment.",
      driftblade: "The fist came down in front of him and I went round his side and out of the wedge and round again, and our knight braced and took it, and I never stopped, not for a breath.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man struck downstage centre, a great wedge of it, at the knight who plays opposite him. The company kept to the wings. (The Doll stood well clear, applauding.)",
      cleric: "The fist fell on our knight, as it always does. I laid the light on him before it landed, and asked the rest of my flock, kindly, to stay out of the wedge. They did. Mostly.",
      grovekeeper: "The fist comes like a rockfall, only in front of him, dear. I'd planted the mending in our knight beforehand, so it was already growing when the stone came down. The rest of my lot kept out of the wedge.",
      chronomancer: "The fist lands a beat after the shoulder drops, a slow beat. I will have warded our knight in the moment before. I did. The rest of us were already outside the wedge, which is where I have always stood.",
      alembist: "Note on the fist: one heavy wedge in front of him, and a ward goes on whoever stands there; everyone else stays out. It also clips the curious. I was curious. Page torn, shoulder sore.",
      threadweaver: "The fist landed on our knight, and I'd tied him to the archer, so it ran down the thread and neither took all of it. The rest of my stitches kept out of the wedge. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Rime Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "It locked onto me and followed. I marched it off the line, away from my friends, and let it fall on empty ground. The sheet cracked after. Off it, boots first, before the water came. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring followed me across the ice like a lantern on a night round. I led it away from the others, and when it fell the sheet split. I stepped off before the black water took it.",
      thresher: "A ring dogs one of us across the ice, settles, falls. I walk mine to the edge, well clear of the others. Then I leave that ground: it cracks and goes under soon after. Poor footing, then.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring chased me round the ice and I wanted to stand in it! I took it away from my friends instead, let it fall, and stepped off the cracked sheet before it sank. Not a scratch. Disappointing.",
      runeguard: "A ring fastened on one of us and followed. The sequence is: lead it from the rest, never across them; it lands; quit that sheet at once, for it cracks and sinks after. I have written this up for my successor in three columns.",
      gorgemaw: "A plate followed me round the table, and I was not to share it. I carried it off to a quiet corner, let it land, and left before the ice under it gave way. Chilled, and not my favourite temperature.",
      oathbroker: "Served on one named party, and it follows them. The fine print forbids delivery to the rest of the undersigned, so lead it clear. The sheet it lands on is forfeit shortly after. Amended accordingly.",
      ironpalm: "A ring followed me. I walked it away from the others. It fell. Then I left the cracked ice before it sank.",
      ranger: "Ring on me, tracking. Walked it clear of the others. Dropped, sheet cracked, sank. Already off it. Thirty paces out is plenty.",
      shadowblade: "A ring followed me, which was flattering. I led it off to a lonely sheet where nobody else stood, let it fall, then slipped away before the ice sank. Pursuit is an art, and I am its subject.",
      pyromancer: "A ring followed me everywhere, like a stage-door admirer! I took it away from the others, let it come down, and left that sheet before it cracked and sank. The adoration was short and sharp.",
      thermist: "Ring on me. Away from the others, then let it lock. Then off that sheet before it sinks. I dawdled once. Too far, too slow. The black water was not my best result.",
      driftblade: "The ring followed me and I ran it out wide, away from everyone, through the open ice, and when it came down I was already off that sheet and away before it sank, and it never touched me.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight followed one of the company across the stage. The trick is to exit with it, away from the others, and to leave that spot after it lands; the boards fall through. (Noted for next season.)",
      cleric: "A ring followed one of my young ones across the ice. She led it away from the rest, let it fall, and stepped off that sheet before it sank, and I thanked her for it after. She learned it quickly.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring followed one of my lot like a hailcloud, dear. They walked it off from the others, let it fall, and left that ground: it cracks and sinks soon after. Nothing grows there, and nothing should.",
      chronomancer: "It follows its quarry, locks, and falls a beat later. Take it from the others, then leave the sheet, for it sinks a beat behind. I will have been clear in time. I was. Mostly in time.",
      alembist: "Observation: a ring follows one of us, then lands. Lead it away from the others, then step off that sheet, for it cracks and sinks soon after. I labelled the sheet. I should not have done it standing on it.",
      threadweaver: "A ring followed one stitch round the floor. I let that thread out long, away from the others, and pulled it back once the ring fell, before the sheet sank. The key is not to pull while it's being sewn.",
    } },
    'Twin Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two marked, a beat between. Apart, both moving, like men crossing a bridge nobody should cross in step. Neither landed on the line. Then off the cracked sheets, no looking back.",
      gloomsworn: "Two rings, two of us, one after the other, like sentries on separate walls. We kept apart and kept walking, and both fell on empty ice. I left those sheets to the water.",
      thresher: "Twice in a row, on two of us, the second hard behind the first. Keep apart, keep walking, and drop them on separate ground, not on each other's. Two frosts, one night. I would have preferred one.",
      bloodreaver: "Two rings! Two of us chased, the second a beat behind. I wished one had been mine, and then it was. We kept apart and kept moving, and then off the cracked ice. I do like being wanted.",
      runeguard: "Two rings, two quarries, the second a beat behind the first; I counted them in the order they closed, which matters. The two keep apart and keep moving, and quit the sheets they crack. Note the order.",
      gorgemaw: "Two plates, two guests, one served a beat after the other. Each carried their own plate to a different corner and kept walking. Leave the cracked table after. Double portions, and I only got half.",
      oathbroker: "Two writs, two parties, the second a beat after the first. The fine print: the two must keep apart, or each pays the other's debt as well. Then quit the cracked sheets. Clause eleven, I think.",
      ironpalm: "Two rings. Two of us. The second a beat behind. We walked apart and did not stop. The ice behind us went quiet.",
      ranger: "Two rings, two of us, one behind the other. Different directions, kept walking. Off the cracks. Done.",
      shadowblade: "The giant chose two of us at once, which was greedy. We went far apart, kept moving, and the second ring fell a beat after the first, on nobody at all. How awkward for it.",
      pyromancer: "Two rings at once, and one of them was mine! I kept well away from the other, kept walking, and dropped mine on bare ice, then fled the crack. My name was on it. I'm only slightly bitter.",
      thermist: "Two rings. Apart. Moving. The second falls a beat behind; don't stop after the first. I stopped. Ran hot at my own stupidity. A good result, in the end.",
      driftblade: "Two rings and one was mine, so I ran it far from the other, round and through the open sheets, and never stopped till both had fallen, and then off the cracks, and nobody collided with anybody.",
      marionettist: "Two spotlights at once, on two of the company, the second a beat behind. They must exit in opposite directions and keep walking. (A duet, poorly blocked the first time. A tolerable second performance.)",
      cleric: "Two of my flock were marked, the second a heartbeat behind the first. I asked them: apart, and keep walking. Dawn keep them. They did, and left the cracked ice after. I was prouder than I should be.",
      grovekeeper: "Two rings came on like a double frost, dear, one behind the other. The two kept apart and kept moving, and both fell on open ground. Then off the cracked sheets. Two seedlings, two seed beds.",
      chronomancer: "The first ring falls, and the second a beat behind it. The two must keep apart and keep moving through both beats. I will have stopped after the first. Once. It was enough to learn it.",
      alembist: "Two rings, two of us, a beat apart. Keep apart, keep moving, quit the cracked sheets. Two flasks, two shelves, no contact between them. I mean it this time, and so do the rings.",
      threadweaver: "Two stitches pulled at once, the second a beat behind. I sent them in opposite directions and kept both moving, so the threads never crossed. Cross two threads and you've a knot nobody enjoys.",
    } },
    'Fault Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A long crack came down the plateau at one of us. Sideways, never along it; every recruit knows to leave a charge by the flank. I would not stand on the red sheets after. I count my footing before I count my wounds.",
      gloomsworn: "A crack ran the length of the plateau toward one of us, wide as a road. I stepped aside, not along it. Each sheet it crossed turned red afterward, and I stayed off every one.",
      thresher: "His fist cuts a long line across the ice at one of us. Step out sideways, never down it. Every sheet it crosses goes red and sinks. I stay off those. A poor night to swim.",
      bloodreaver: "A great crack drove right at me, across the whole plateau! I stepped sideways out of it, and the ice it crossed went red and sank. A good line. Not a good place to stand.",
      runeguard: "His fist came down and a long wide line was drawn at one of us. Step out sideways, never along it; then shun every sheet marked red, for they sink. I have underlined the second half, since the first half is obvious.",
      gorgemaw: "Carved the whole table in one stroke, a long cut toward one guest. I stepped aside, not along the knife, and kept off every plate it marked red. They sink. Poor carving, honestly.",
      oathbroker: "A writ drawn across the whole estate at one party. Step out sideways. The fine print is the red: every sheet it crosses is forfeit, and sinks. We did not return to them. Entered.",
      ironpalm: "He struck the ice and a long line came at one of us. I stepped aside. Then I kept to the white ice and off the red.",
      ranger: "Long wide lane across the plateau, aimed at one of us. Out to the side, not down it. Everything it crosses goes red. Stayed off the red.",
      shadowblade: "A crack drove across the whole plateau at someone. Not me, naturally; I stepped sideways out of it anyway. The sheets it marked red are no place for a lady. It never saw me leave.",
      pyromancer: "A long crack came racing across the ice straight at me! I stepped out sideways without dropping a spell, and kept off every red sheet it left behind. Theatrical, but I'd have been theatrical about it too.",
      thermist: "Line: long, wide, at one of us. Sideways out. Hot read, once past: every red sheet sinks, stay off them. I didn't, once. Black water. Cold fury. Too far, in every sense.",
      driftblade: "The fault came across the plateau at me and I slipped out the side of it, not down it, and through the gap, and kept off every red sheet it had touched, never landing on one, not once.",
      marionettist: "A long crack split the stage toward one of the company. Out sideways, never along it. Every board it crosses is marked red and gives way. (The Doll stepped neatly round them. Applause.)",
      cleric: "A long crack came across the ice at one of my flock. I called to her: out to the side, never along it. Every sheet it crossed was marked red and sank. We kept off them, every one.",
      grovekeeper: "A long fault opened like a gully after rain, straight at one of my lot. Out sideways, dear, and don't go back. Everything it crossed went red and sank. Bad ground, that, until it settles.",
      chronomancer: "The line is drawn, then struck a beat later. Out of it sideways on the first beat. The sheets it crosses sink a few beats after that; I will have left them already. I had.",
      alembist: "Note, in red ink: a long wide line across the plateau at one of us. Out sideways. Every sheet it crosses is marked red and sinks after. Do not set anything down there. I set something down there.",
      threadweaver: "A long rip ran across the whole cloth at one stitch. Out sideways, never along the tear. Every sheet it crossed went red, and the threads stayed off those. Mend the tear later, not while it's open.",
    } },
    'Icefall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Hailstorm. No post to hold in a hailstorm, nothing to raise a shield against when the shards fall from above. I kept marching and stepped out of every circle. Standing still was the only way to fail.",
      gloomsworn: "Shards dropped out of the lights like stars in the small hours, small rings, two of them on us. I kept walking and stepped out of every ring. A sentry who stands still gets buried.",
      thresher: "Ice came down like hail, small rings all over, two of them on us. Hail ruins whatever stands still, so I kept moving and stepped clear of each one. A great many have been lost that way.",
      bloodreaver: "Ice raining down! Little rings everywhere, two of them right on us. I kept moving and stepped out of each one. Small cuts, barely worth the name. I almost lingered in a ring to check.",
      runeguard: "Small rings bloom beneath the lights, two upon us, each answered by a shard a moment later. Keep moving; step out of every ring before the shard lands. It is the only sequence I can recommend without footnotes.",
      gorgemaw: "Crushed ice sprinkled over the whole table, little rings of it, two on the guests. I moved along and kept out of each. Garnish, nothing more. I would have liked a sauce.",
      oathbroker: "A series of small claims against named ground, two against the undersigned in person. Keep moving; clear each ring in turn. None lodged against us stood.",
      ironpalm: "Ice fell, in small rings. Two on us. I stepped out of each and did not stop. Hail does not wait for us to be ready.",
      ranger: "Shards from the sky. Small rings, two on us. Kept moving, stepped out of each. Open ground, few places to hide, no need to.",
      shadowblade: "Ice fell out of the lights in small rings, two over us. I kept moving, always between the rings, never under one. Hail has no manners at all.",
      pyromancer: "It hailed! Little rings everywhere, two right on us, and I had to keep moving, which I loathe in the middle of a spell. I stepped out of every one. Only slightly bruised, and nobody saw.",
      thermist: "Shards. Small rings. Two on us. Step out, keep moving. I ran cold and careful the whole time, a rare combination for me, and stayed between the rings. Result: no hits. I intend to write it down.",
      driftblade: "Ice came down out of the lights in little rings and I ran between them, through the gaps, out of the two that wanted me, never landing, never stopping, never even slowing.",
      marionettist: "Ice fell from the flies in small circles, two of them on members of the company. The company kept moving and stepped out of each. (The Doll caught one. Unhurt, though it complained.)",
      cleric: "Shards fell from the sky, small rings everywhere, two on my flock. I told them: keep moving, step out of every ring you see. Nobody stood still, and nobody had to be carried.",
      grovekeeper: "It came like spring hail, dear: small rings everywhere, two on my lot. Keep moving and step out of each one. Hail passes; it only hurts the ones who stand.",
      chronomancer: "Each shard lands a beat after its ring appears. I will have stepped out of every one on its beat. I kept the rhythm, mostly, and hummed it, which helped.",
      alembist: "Ice came down in small rings, two on us, and nothing left on the floor survived. Moving is the only cure. I left a flask on the floor. It was a good flask. Entry closed.",
      threadweaver: "Ice fell like dropped pins, small rings all over, two on my stitches. Keep moving, step out of each. Pins always find the one who stops to look.",
    } },
    "Titan's Sweep": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A huge slow arc across his front, meant for one of us. Big, but not a full circle. I took the line round to his flank and his back. A slow blow is honest about where it is going.",
      gloomsworn: "The giant swept a vast slow arc before him, turned toward one of us. It does not reach round behind him. I took the watch to his side, out of the arc, and let it pass.",
      thresher: "He swings a huge slow arc in front of him, a scythe twice the length of mine, aimed at one of us. It does not reach his sides or back. I went round to his flank. I know scythes.",
      bloodreaver: "A sweep wide as a field, aimed at someone else! I wanted to stand in it, I admit. I went to his side instead, out of the arc. It does not reach behind him. Dull ground, to be fair.",
      runeguard: "A great arc drawn before him, turned on one of us; wide, but only a part of the circle. Go to his side or behind him. The arc is carved large and slow, so one has time to read it. I read it twice.",
      gorgemaw: "One huge sweep cleared the front of the table, aimed at one guest. Those at his elbow or behind him were untouched. I moved my chair. Generous portion, poorly aimed.",
      oathbroker: "A broad claim turned on one party, covering much of the ground before him. The fine print is the omission: it does not reach his sides or back. We stood there. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "He swept a great arc before him. I went to his side. It passed.",
      ranger: "Huge arc in front, turned at one of us. Not a full circle. His flank or his back. Slow enough to walk round.",
      shadowblade: "He swept the whole front of the plateau, which was dramatic of him. I was behind, of course, where the arc doesn't reach. A mark should keep its back covered.",
      pyromancer: "He swept the whole front of the plateau in one slow arc! I hurried round to his side, which ruined my spell, and I was right to. It does not reach behind him. Grudging respect.",
      thermist: "Arc. Huge, slow, in front. Only part of the circle. To his side, then, and back in behind him cutting at his heels. Result: untouched. Frankly I expected to be flattened.",
      driftblade: "The arc came round in front of him, huge and slow, and I ran round his side and through behind him and out the far flank, never in it, and it was so slow I could have run it twice.",
      marionettist: "A sweep across the whole front of the stage, aimed at one of the company. The company exited to the wings and behind him, where the arc does not reach. (Superb blocking.)",
      cleric: "A great slow arc in front of him, turned toward one of my flock. I called to them: to his side, or behind him; it is wide but it does not go all the way round. They went, and were glad.",
      grovekeeper: "The sweep comes like a slow gale across the front of him, dear, and aimed at one of my lot. My lot went to his side and behind him, where the wind doesn't reach. Stand where the weather isn't.",
      chronomancer: "The arc is drawn long before it falls, and it falls slowly. I will have been at his side already, out of it. It covers most of the front and none of the back. Sensible planning, on his part.",
      alembist: "Label for the shelf: front of him unsafe, back of him excellent. The sweep is huge and slow, aimed at one of us, and not a full circle. To his side or behind. I reached it with all my vials still unbroken, which is a first.",
      threadweaver: "The arc swept the front of the floor like shears across a bolt, aimed at one stitch. I pulled every thread to his side and behind him, out of the cut. A clean cut, but not our cloth.",
    } },
    "Winter's Grasp": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Frost under a couple of us, and the sheet froze. Frozen ice will not crack under his blows, but it is slick as a wet barracks floor. I stepped on boots flat and with room to spare.",
      gloomsworn: "Frost rose from the feet of a couple of us, and the ice under each thickened and went glassy while the old frost thawed. Frozen ice will not crack, but it slides. I stepped on slowly, never toward the water.",
      thresher: "Frost creeps out from under a couple of us and freezes the sheet where it lands; the old frost lets go. Frozen ice will not crack, it only slides, so step on gently and never toward the water. A polished floor, almost.",
      bloodreaver: "Frost gripped a couple of us at the feet, and the sheet under each froze hard and glassy. His blows glance off frozen ice; good. But it slides. I stepped on toward nothing that bites. Bring back the bite.",
      runeguard: "Frost gathered at the feet of a couple of us, and the sheet beneath each froze while the old frost thawed. Frozen ice will not crack, but it is slick, so step on with room to spare. Recorded without drama.",
      gorgemaw: "Frost crept from under a couple of my fellow guests and glazed the sheet beneath each, like sugar on a pastry. Glazed plates do not crack, but they slide. I stepped on carefully. Crisp. Almost sweet.",
      oathbroker: "The Grasp converts the ground under a couple of parties into frozen ice, and the old frost thaws. Fine print: frozen sheets cannot be cracked, but stepping onto one carries you further than you meant. Noted for the record.",
      ironpalm: "Frost rose under a couple of us. The ice froze thick. It will not crack. It slides. I stepped on slowly, away from the water.",
      ranger: "Frost under a couple of us, sheet freezes. Old frost thaws. Frozen won't crack, but it slides. Step on with room, never toward water.",
      shadowblade: "Frost crept from under a couple of us and glazed the sheets, and the old ones thawed. Glazed ice does not crack, but it slides. I stepped on gracefully, away from the water. Mostly.",
      pyromancer: "Frost came up out of the ice under me, which was an insult, and froze the sheet like glass! It will not crack, but I slid across it like a dropped plate. Step on gently. Dignity optional.",
      thermist: "Frost under a couple of us. The sheet freezes; the old frost thaws. Frozen holds. Frozen slides. I ran too hot onto it and skated twice the distance I meant. Slowly, next time. Slowly, slowly.",
      driftblade: "Frost reached up from under a couple of us and froze the sheets like glass, and I ran onto one and it carried me further than I meant, so now I run on with room, and never toward the water.",
      marionettist: "Frost crept out from beneath a couple of the company and glazed the boards under them. Glazed boards will not break, but they slide. The company now makes its entrances slowly. (The Doll skated. Charming.)",
      cleric: "Frost curled up under two of my flock and froze their sheets, while the old frost let go. It will not break now, only slide. I asked them to step on gently, and away from the water.",
      grovekeeper: "Frost came up out of the ground under a couple of my lot, dear, the way hoarfrost comes overnight. Frozen sheets will not crack, but they slide. The old frost thaws. Walk on soft-footed.",
      chronomancer: "The frost lands a beat after it gathers, and the sheet under each freezes; the old thaws a beat later. Frozen will not crack. Frozen slides. I will have stepped on carefully. I slid.",
      alembist: "Frost from the feet of a couple of us, and the sheet freezes while the old frost thaws. Frozen sheets resist his blows but are slick as a spilled tincture. Step on with room. Store accordingly, and do not run.",
      threadweaver: "Frost came up from under a few stitches and glazed the cloth beneath each. Glazed cloth won't tear, but it slips. Step on with room to spare, and leave the tension loose.",
    } },
    'Frostbite': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One of ours went slow and blue, a ring trailing behind. We marched him to the nearest brazier, kept the road clear, and the fire drew the cold out. Nobody on my line gets left in the cold.",
      gloomsworn: "The cold took one of us and slowed him, a ring to share trailing behind. I walked him to the nearest brazier and kept the way clear of anything else. The fire drew it out. I know what the other end of that road looks like.",
      thresher: "One of us catches the frost proper: slow, then stiff as a statue, with a ring trailing behind. The nearest brazier thaws them. Walk them there, clear the way, and do not dawdle. Nobody wants a gallery of frozen friends.",
      bloodreaver: "The cold got into one of us and slowed him to a crawl, a ring trailing behind. I carried him to the nearest brazier and the fire took it out. Warmth is cheap. I just wish it were blood.",
      runeguard: "One of us was chilled, slowed, a ring to share trailing after. The remedy is the nearest brazier: a moment's warmth. The rest of us cleared the road to it. Untreated, the entry in my ledger reads frozen solid.",
      gorgemaw: "One of my fellow guests went cold and slow, a ring trailing after like a bad aftertaste. Nearest brazier, a moment's warmth, and the way kept clear. Neither, and he sets like jelly. I escorted him myself.",
      oathbroker: "A small claim on one party: slowed, a ring to share following. The remedy is the nearest brazier, a moment's warmth; the undersigned walk with them and clear the route. Failing that, they freeze. Entered.",
      ironpalm: "One of us was chilled and slowed, a ring following. I walked beside him to the nearest brazier. Warmth, a moment. If the fire is too far, share the ring and share the cold.",
      ranger: "One of us chilled. Slowed, ring following. Walk them to the nearest brazier, clear the route. If they can't make it, stand in the ring with them. Cold doesn't care about aim.",
      shadowblade: "One of us went slow and cold, a ring following. I helped carry the poor darling to the nearest brazier, clearing the route, and the fire did the rest. Dignity, I'm told, comes back with the colour.",
      pyromancer: "One of us got chilled, slowed to a crawl, and I was the warmest candidate present! I could not light the brazier myself, as it turns out, but I walked them to it and cleared the road. Slow, but a very dramatic rescue.",
      thermist: "Frostbite! Slowed, a ring trailing. Nearest brazier, a moment's warmth, route clear. If too far, share the ring. I shared it twice. Warm results either way, which is rare.",
      driftblade: "One of us got slowed to a crawl by the cold, which for me is the worst thing to hear, and a ring followed them, so we ran ahead, cleared the way to the nearest brazier, and walked them in. Somebody has to hurry for them.",
      marionettist: "One of the company took a chill: slowed, a ring trailing after like a bad review. The nearest brazier thaws them. We cleared the way, played the scene slowly, and arrived in time. (Stage left, a warm exit.)",
      cleric: "One of my flock went cold and slow, a ring trailing behind him. I walked him to the nearest brazier and the rest cleared the way; the fire drew the cold out. If he cannot reach it, we share the ring with him. Nobody left alone.",
      grovekeeper: "Frost got into one of my lot, dear, slowing him to a crawl with a ring behind. The nearest brazier is the cure; I walked him there while the rest cleared the way. Frost, left alone, finishes the job.",
      chronomancer: "One of us slowed, a ring trailing behind. The brazier cures it, but it must be reached before the freeze finishes. We cleared a path to the nearest and walked him in. I will have been quicker next time. I was.",
      alembist: "One of us, slowed, a ring to share: the nearest brazier, a moment's warmth. Fallback: all share the ring. Untreated: frozen solid, and I have no label for that.",
      threadweaver: "One stitch went cold and slow, a ring trailing behind. I walked her to the nearest brazier, cleared the thread, and the fire drew the cold out of her. If she can't reach it, we all sew into the ring together.",
    } },
    'Whiteout': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "White over all of us, and nothing to dodge. We were mended before it came. When it cleared, every sunk sheet was ice again and I had ground under my boots. The first fair thing he did.",
      gloomsworn: "A white blast took every one of us at once, and the dark had nothing to say against it. We were whole before it came. When it cleared, every sunk sheet was ice again. The dead do not mind a good winter.",
      thresher: "A big white sweep that finds every one of us, a drift pouring over a wall. Be whole before it comes. After, it is kinder: every sunk sheet is ice again, and the floor is fit to walk.",
      bloodreaver: "A blinding white blast that found every one of us, and I laughed into it. Not a bad hit! We were mended before it came, and afterwards every sunk sheet was ice again. Fair trade.",
      runeguard: "The Whiteout reaches all and no ward turns it, so each must be whole before it sounds. Its one grace, recorded here: when it clears, every sunk sheet is ice again and the floor is restored to us.",
      gorgemaw: "A great white course served to all of us together, no one to eat it on another's behalf. We came to table mended. After, the missing sheets were whole again, which is the proper way to clear a table.",
      oathbroker: "A levy on all, payable at once. The mercy of it is in the fine print: after, every sunk sheet is restored to ice, and the estate is whole again. A debt that pays itself back is a rare thing.",
      ironpalm: "White, over all of us. There was no stepping aside. We came to it whole. After, every sunk sheet was ice again, and the floor spoke kindly.",
      ranger: "Whiteout. Everyone, no cover. Came to it mended. After, every sunk sheet is ice again. Back to the shooting.",
      shadowblade: "A white blast and not a shadow in it, which offended me. We were all whole when it came. When it cleared, the sunk sheets were ice again, the poor things. A kind afterthought, for a giant.",
      pyromancer: "A blinding blast of white that took every one of us, and for a moment I was the only thing glowing! We were mended before it came, and after it, every sunk sheet was solid ice again. My eyebrows will recover.",
      thermist: "Whiteout. Cold on all of us, blinding. Be whole before it. Then, after: every sunk sheet is ice again. A reset, and I do enjoy a reset. My teeth are still talking about it.",
      driftblade: "A white blast went through everyone and I couldn't outrun it, which is rude, so I came to it whole and then, when it cleared, every sunk sheet was ice again and I could run where I liked.",
      marionettist: "A blinding white curtain fell across the whole house. No exits, nowhere to hide; the company arrived whole and sat through it. When it lifted, every sunken board was restored. (A rare kindness from management.)",
      cleric: "A white blast reached every one of us, blinding. I had my flock mended before it came, as I ought, and afterwards every sunk sheet was ice again. A mercy I had not expected from him.",
      grovekeeper: "A great white wind took all of my lot, dear, blinding as a blizzard. Plant the mending beforehand. When it cleared, every sunk sheet was ice again, the way a lake freezes after the thaw.",
      chronomancer: "The whiteout lands on everyone, and I had the mending in place a moment before. After it, every sunk sheet is ice again, as if the whole lake had been unsunk. I do like an undoing.",
      alembist: "Whiteout, felt by all. Pour the strong flask in advance and have everyone whole before it sounds. Side effect: every sunk sheet restored to ice. A welcome reaction. I shall note it in green.",
      threadweaver: "A blinding white seam of cold, and it ran through every thread. We were all whole before it came. After, the sunk sheets came up again as ice, like a hem let down. Neat, for once.",
    } },
    'The Long Night': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lights low, and for a long while slow arcs from him, with frost called twice. Keep out of the arcs; whoever the frost marks chooses the ice by where they stand. I chose a sound post and stayed on it.",
      gloomsworn: "The lights dimmed and the plateau went truly dark, and I felt very nearly at home. Slow arcs from him, frost called twice. We stayed out of the arcs; whoever the frost chose set the ice by where they stood.",
      thresher: "The lights went low and he swung one slow arc after another, the longest night there is, with frost called twice besides. Keep clear of the arcs. Whoever the frost marks lays the ice by where they stand.",
      bloodreaver: "Dark, and a long night of slow arcs and frost called twice! I kept clear of the arcs, though it hurt not to stand in them. When the frost marked me, I picked my sheet by where I stood. Dull fight, tense nerves.",
      runeguard: "The lights dimmed; for a long time he swung great slow arcs, and frost was called twice. I kept clear of the arcs, as the sequence demands, while whoever the frost marked set the ice by where they stood. Preparation, not climax.",
      gorgemaw: "The lights dimmed and the cook swung great slow arcs, one after another, calling frost twice. We kept out of the arcs. Those the frost marked chose their sheet by where they stood. A long, patient course.",
      oathbroker: "The drafting period: frost called twice, and whoever it marks chooses where the ice will lie by where they stand. The fine print: keep out of his arcs while drafting. A hurried clause is a bad clause.",
      ironpalm: "The lights went dim. He spoke in great slow arcs, and we kept out of them. When the frost marked me, I chose my sheet by where I stood, and stood well.",
      ranger: "Dark. Slow arcs, frost twice. Out of the arcs. When the frost marked me, picked my sheet by where I stood.",
      shadowblade: "The lights dimmed and he swung slow arcs in the dark, as if he needed the privacy. I stayed out of them. When the frost marked me, I chose my sheet by where I stood. A little dark suits me.",
      pyromancer: "The lights dimmed and I was suddenly the brightest thing on the plateau! He swung great slow arcs and called the frost twice. I kept clear, and when it marked me I chose my sheet by where I stood. A very long night.",
      thermist: "Dark. Cold. Slow arcs, frost twice. Out of the arcs. The marked choose their sheet by where they stand, and I stood too close to the edge once and learned something unpleasant.",
      driftblade: "The lights dimmed and he swung great slow arcs for a long, long time, which for me was a gift, and frost was called twice, and when it marked me I picked my sheet by where I stood and ran there.",
      marionettist: "The house lights fell and our leading man swung long slow arcs through the dark, while the frost was called twice. The company kept out of the arcs; the marked chose their sheet by where they stood. (A long second act.)",
      cleric: "The lights dimmed and I did not like the look of it. Slow arcs one after another, and the frost called twice. I kept my flock out of the arcs, and asked the marked ones to choose where they stood with care. Hot tea after, I think.",
      grovekeeper: "The lights dimmed, dear, and it was the longest night, slow arcs and frost called twice. We stayed out of the arcs; the marked chose their sheet by where they stood. Seeds are laid in the dark.",
      chronomancer: "A long dim stretch, slow arcs, frost twice. Out of the arcs. The marked set the ice by where they stand. Every beat felt like three. I will have stood in the right place both times.",
      alembist: "Dimmed lights, great slow arcs, frost called twice. Out of the arcs; the frost-marked choose the sheet by where they stand. Preparation for later. Bring a candle.",
      threadweaver: "The lights dimmed and he swung slow arcs for a good long while, with frost called twice. We stayed out of the cuts; whoever the frost marked chose the sheet by where they stood. Put the stitches where you can see them.",
    } },
    'Avalanche': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The plateau tilted at the end and snow came in lanes. Whatever was not frozen was marked red. I had the line on frozen ground before the first lane. Wet to the knees afterward, but standing. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The plateau tilted and snow poured down in lanes, one after another. Whatever was not frozen was marked red. I stood the watch on a frozen sheet before the first lane. I have been buried before and did not care to repeat it.",
      thresher: "The whole floor tips and the drift comes down in lanes, one after the other. Only the frozen sheets hold; the rest are marked red. Get onto a frozen one before the first lane. There is no room to dither.",
      bloodreaver: "The plateau tipped and snow thundered down in lanes, one after another! Every sheet not frozen was marked red. We got up on the frozen ones before the first lane. I'd have liked to get hit by it, to be honest.",
      runeguard: "The plateau tipped and the snow ran in lanes, one after another. Every sheet not frozen was marked red. The sequence is simple: be upon a frozen sheet before the first lane. Everything the Titan prepared leads here.",
      gorgemaw: "At the end of the course the whole table tilted and the snow poured down in lanes, one after another. Only the frozen plates held; the rest were marked red. We took our seats on the frozen ones early. A grand finish.",
      oathbroker: "The final settlement: the snow comes in lanes, one after another, and every sheet not frozen is marked red. The fine print is that the frozen ones are safe. The undersigned stood upon them before the first lane.",
      ironpalm: "The plateau tipped. Snow came in lanes, one after another. The sheets not frozen were red. We stood on the frozen ones before the first lane and let it pass over.",
      ranger: "Plateau tips. Snow in lanes, one after another. Every sheet not frozen is red. All of us on frozen ice before the first lane.",
      shadowblade: "The plateau tipped and the snow came down in lanes, like a long curtain. Every sheet not frozen was marked red. I was on a frozen one before the first lane, of course, and nobody knew when I'd arrived.",
      pyromancer: "The whole plateau tilted and the snow came down in lanes, one after another, absolutely magnificent! Everything not frozen was marked red. I was on the frozen ones before the first lane. My best sprint yet.",
      thermist: "Plateau tips! Lanes of snow, one after another. Cold read: every unfrozen sheet is red. Hot read: be on a frozen one before the first lane, right at the edge of the cold. I made it with a hair to spare. Singed my pride.",
      driftblade: "The plateau tilted and snow came down in lanes, one after another, and I ran for a frozen sheet before the first lane, through the slide and onto the glass, and it was the best run of my life, and I never fell.",
      marionettist: "The stage tilted and the snow came down in lanes, one after another, and every board not frozen was marked red. The company took their places on frozen boards before the first lane. (A thunderous finale.)",
      cleric: "At the end the plateau tipped and snow came down in lanes, one after another. Every sheet not frozen was marked red. I called everyone to the frozen ones before the first lane. They came without arguing, which is rare.",
      grovekeeper: "The hillside slips at the end, dear, and the snow comes down in lanes, one after another. Everything not frozen is marked red. My lot stood on frozen ground before the first lane. Frozen earth holds the way roots do.",
      chronomancer: "The plateau tips, and the snow comes down in lanes, one after another, each a beat behind the last. Every sheet not frozen is red. We stood on frozen ones before the first lane. I would have been there sooner, but I wasn't.",
      alembist: "The plateau tips and the snow comes in lanes, one after another, every sheet not frozen marked red. Everyone on frozen sheets before the first lane. Prepared earlier, I hope. I put my flasks in my pockets, just in case.",
      threadweaver: "The plateau tipped and the snow came down in lanes, one behind the other, like a bolt unrolled. Every sheet not frozen was marked red. I pulled every stitch onto a frozen sheet before the first lane. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Glacial Stomp': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He stamped, and two rings of shards ran out behind it. Right after the foot landed I stepped under him, into the quiet circle at his heels. Closest was safest. I would never have believed it on the parade ground.",
      gloomsworn: "A stamp, then two rings of shards sweep out from him, one chasing the other. Right after the foot lands, the ring at his feet is calm. I went in close beside him and let them roll over. Even I hide from some things.",
      thresher: "He stamps, and two rings of shards roll out from his feet like ripples through still water. Right after the foot lands the circle beneath him is quiet, so I stepped in and let both pass over me. The eye of the storm. Closest seat in the house.",
      bloodreaver: "A stomp at his feet, then two rings of shards racing out! I stepped right in under him once the foot had landed and let both rings pass over. Closest I've been to his ankles. Lovely, and not a mark.",
      runeguard: "His foot lifts, and the sign is plain. Let it fall, then step at once into the quiet circle at his feet; the two rings of shards run outward, one after the other, and pass over. The safest place was beside the one who made it.",
      gorgemaw: "He stamped at the head of the table, and two rings of shards rolled outward. Once his foot had landed, I stepped to his feet and let both pass. The best seat is closest to the cook. Always.",
      oathbroker: "The Stomp lands at his feet and two rings of shards follow, outward, one after the other. The fine print exempts the circle at his feet once the foot has fallen. We stepped in, and the clause held. Surety given.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped. Two rings ran out, one after the other. Once the foot had fallen, the circle beneath him was still. I stepped into the stillness and let both pass.",
      ranger: "Stomp at his feet. Then two rings of shards running out. Once the foot lands, step in close. Rings pass over. Closest cover on the field.",
      shadowblade: "He stamped, and rings of shards ran out from his feet, one after the other. Once the foot was down, I slipped in close, under his chin, and let them pass. Tucked in tight, where he never looks.",
      pyromancer: "He stamped and two rings of shards came racing out! I learned to wait for the foot to land, then step right into the quiet circle at his feet and let them pass. Two rings, no singe. Well, no frost.",
      thermist: "Stomp! Ring, ring, one after another. Wait for the foot to land, then into the quiet circle at his feet, right under his chin. The one spot with no weather at all.",
      driftblade: "He stamped, and two rings of shards came running out one after the other, and for once I stopped, and stepped into the quiet circle at his feet and let them go through and over me, and it felt wrong, and it worked.",
      marionettist: "Centre stage, a stamp, and two rings of shards roll out to the wings. Once his foot has fallen, the company steps in close beside him. (The Doll, for once, stood centre stage.)",
      cleric: "He stomped, and two rings of shards ran out one after the other. I told my flock: wait for the foot to land, then step in close to him and let the rings pass over. It feels like stepping into the lion's den. It is the safest place there is.",
      grovekeeper: "He stamps and the rings of shards come out like ripples, one after the other, dear. Once the foot has landed, the ground at his feet is still. Plant yourself there. The eye of the storm is a very quiet field.",
      chronomancer: "The stamp lands, then two rings, one after the other. Right after the foot lands, the circle at his feet is calm. I stepped in on the beat. Early, you're struck; late, you've missed the lull.",
      alembist: "A stomp, then two rings of shards outward. Once the foot lands, step into the quiet circle at his feet and let the rings pass. Not before. Learned the hard way, in the margin, in smoke.",
      threadweaver: "He stomped, then two rings of shards ran outward. Once the foot was down I pulled every stitch in close to him, and the rings passed over the hem. Draw the thread in tight, then let it go slack.",
    } },
    'Breath of Winter': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A wedge of frost at one of us. It hardly hurt. But whatever it crossed froze and, a moment later, broke and sank, so I had the line off that ground before it went.",
      gloomsworn: "He breathed frost at one of us. It barely hurt, which I took personally. Whatever it crossed froze, then broke and sank a few moments later. I got the watch off it before it went.",
      thresher: "His breath freezes a wedge of floor, aimed at one of us. It hardly stings. But every sheet it crosses freezes, breaks, and goes under. Get off those before they break. A poor night to wade.",
      bloodreaver: "His breath went into one of us in a wedge of frost, barely a scratch, shameful! But every sheet it crossed froze and then broke and sank. Disappointing hit, treacherous floor. I got off before the break.",
      runeguard: "A wedge of frost, aimed at one of us, a small claim with a large rider: every sheet it crosses freezes, then breaks and sinks a few moments later. The rider is the point. Quit those sheets.",
      gorgemaw: "His breath is a cold little cone of garnish, aimed at one guest, barely a mouthful. But every plate it touches freezes, then breaks and sinks. Quit those sheets, and quickly. A sneaky dish.",
      oathbroker: "A small claim with a large rider. The breath itself is slight; the fine print is that every sheet it crosses freezes, then breaks and sinks. Quit the sheets before the rider is called in. Noted.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed out, aimed at one of us. It was little. The ice it crossed froze, then broke and sank. I stepped away from it before it spoke again.",
      ranger: "Cone of frost at one of us. Little sting. Every sheet it crosses freezes, then breaks and sinks. Get off those sheets fast.",
      shadowblade: "He breathed on one of us, the rudest thing, a cone of frost. It hardly stung. Every sheet it crossed froze, then broke and sank. I was somewhere else entirely by then.",
      pyromancer: "He breathed frost across the ice in a long wedge, at one of us, and it barely tickled! But every sheet it crossed froze and then broke and sank. I got off them before the break. My sleeve would not have survived it.",
      thermist: "Breath! Cold, a cone, at one of us, a mild sting. Then the hot read: every sheet it crosses freezes, then breaks and sinks, and I was still standing on one. A leap. Safe. Heart going like a kettle.",
      driftblade: "His breath came across the ice in a wedge at one of us, and it hardly stung, but every sheet it crossed froze and then broke and sank, so I didn't stop to be impressed, I just ran off every one before the break.",
      marionettist: "A cone of frost, aimed at one of the company. It barely hurt, but every board it crossed froze, then broke and sank. (Exit, at speed, before the floor goes.)",
      cleric: "He breathed frost at one of my flock, and it hardly hurt. But every sheet it crossed froze, then snapped and sank a few moments later. I called them off those sheets in good time. The gentle ones are often the worst.",
      grovekeeper: "His breath came like a late frost, dear, light on the skin. But every sheet it crossed froze hard, then broke and sank. My lot stepped off before the break. Quiet frosts do the most damage to the ground.",
      chronomancer: "The breath hurts little. The ground it crossed freezes, then breaks and sinks a few beats later. I will have left it already. I did, somewhere around the second beat, and it was enough.",
      alembist: "Mild symptoms, severe side effects. A cone of frost at one of us that barely stings, and every sheet it crosses freezes, then breaks and sinks. Leave those sheets before they break. I did not read the label first.",
      threadweaver: "His breath came at one stitch, light as lace. But every sheet it crossed froze, then broke and sank. I pulled the thread off those sheets before the break. The lace is the trap, not the cloth.",
    } },
    "Titan's Grip": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A ring followed one of us. Anyone in it when it closed was held fast, and a ring came up on them to share. The whole line ran to the held man and stood in it. Otherwise, out of the first ring. Nobody is held alone.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring followed one of us, and the fist closed on whoever stood in it. A ring to share came up on the held one, and we all went to stand in it. A man who has been held in the dark knows nobody leaves him there.",
      thresher: "A ring follows one of us, and whoever is in it when it shuts is taken in the fist and held. Then a ring comes up on them, and everyone shuffles in to share it. Else keep out of the first, or be crushed.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring followed one of us, and the fist closed on whoever stood in it! If it caught you, a ring came up for the rest of us to share. I ran straight into it. Squeezing me is a poor idea.",
      runeguard: "A ring follows one of us; whoever is within when it closes is seized and held, and a ring to share rises on them. The answer is that every one of us goes to stand in it, or the held one is crushed. Otherwise, keep out of the first.",
      gorgemaw: "A ring followed one guest and the fist closed on anyone in it, holding them like a prize turkey. A ring to share came up and we all piled in beside them. Otherwise keep out of the first. Never leave a guest in the fist.",
      oathbroker: "The Grip is an arrest: whoever is inside the ring when it closes is held, and a shared debt appears on them. The fine print: all of the undersigned run in and pay their share, or the held party is crushed. Otherwise, stay out.",
      ironpalm: "A ring followed one of us. Whoever stood in it was held, and a ring to share came upon them. We ran to it, all of us, and stood together. Otherwise, we kept out of the first.",
      ranger: "Ring tracks one of us. Inside when it closes: seized, share ring on them. Everyone runs in. Otherwise keep out. Don't leave them alone in there.",
      shadowblade: "A ring followed someone, and the fist closed on whoever stood in it. A ring to share came up on the poor thing and we all ran in. I don't often run toward a mark, but there it is. Otherwise, keep well clear of the first ring.",
      pyromancer: "A ring followed one of us and the fist closed on whoever stood in it! Held, and a ring to share on them, so I ran straight in with everyone else, purely for company. I hate being held. I hate more being left.",
      thermist: "Grip! A ring on one of us; whoever's in it when it closes is held. Don't be in it. If caught, the ring to share: everybody in, now. Share the load. Too far from the middle and they're crushed.",
      driftblade: "A ring followed one of us and the fist closed on whoever stood in it, and I really didn't like that, being held, but a ring to share came up and the whole party ran in, and I ran in fastest.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight followed one of the company and the fist closed upon whoever stood in it. A ring to share came up on the held player and the company flooded in. (Never leave a player alone on stage.) Otherwise, stay clear of the first ring.",
      cleric: "A ring followed one of my flock, and the fist closed on whoever stood in it. A ring to share came up on the held one, and I called everyone in. A shared burden is lighter. Otherwise, keep out of the first ring.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring followed one of my lot, dear, and whoever stood in it when it closed was held fast. A ring to share came up on them, and the whole lot ran in together. If nobody goes, they are crushed. Keep out of the first ring otherwise.",
      chronomancer: "A ring follows one of us. Whoever is in it when it closes is held, and a ring to share comes up. Everyone in, before the crush, a beat early if possible. Otherwise stay out. I will have stayed out. I did.",
      alembist: "A ring follows; whoever is inside when it closes is held, and a ring to share appears. Everyone into it, or they are crushed. Otherwise keep out. No exceptions, and, for once, no spills.",
      threadweaver: "A ring followed one stitch. When it closed, he gripped whoever was inside, and a ring to share came up on them. Every thread drew in tight around the held one, or they're crushed. Otherwise keep out of the first ring.",
    } },
  },
};
